          heading: "Common mistakes and misconceptions",
          kind: "warning",
          body:
            "- **Fixing before mitigating.** Root cause at 2 a.m. is a hypothesis with bad lighting; rollback/flag/limit first, diagnose calmly after. You can always mitigate faster than fix.\n+- **No incident commander.** Multiple responders making independent changes produces incident #2 and lost timelines. IC coordinates, doesn't code.\n+- **Untimed, unscribed response.** The review a week later is folklore without minute-zero records. Scribe from the start; mark decisions and hypotheses.\n+- **Fixing forward under pressure.** An unreviewed \"quick fix\" deployed mid-incident is the next incident. Mitigate with rehearsed levers (rollback, flags); fixes go through the pipeline.\n+- **Trying to roll back the database.** Expand-contract means schema stays compatible with old code — write \"no destructive DDL during incidents\" into the runbook *before* you need it.\n+- **Silence during incidents.** Stakeholders improvise; users guess; channels go rogue. Cadence comms from the declaration, even with no news.\n+- **Blameless as a slogan.** If reviews name individuals as causes, reports get hidden. Test: new-joiner safety; construct counterfactuals from *system* facts only.\n+- **\"Be more careful\" as an action item.** Not a system change — a promise of omniscience. Every why must reach a default/check/rule/automation.\n+- **Many items, no dates, no owners.** Ten abandoned actions teach the team reviews are theatre. Few, owned, dated, tracked at the retro.\n+- **Skipping \"what went well\".** Unpraised defences (the auto-halt, the rehearsal) get eroded by the next refactor. Name them; protect them.\n+- **Forgetting detection as an action class.** Reviews that fix code but leave the blind spot that let it happen. MTTD improvements are deliverables.\n+- **Error budget burned, no consequence.** The policy is what funds fixes: freeze risky change while the budget is empty — or the number is decoration.\n+- **Measuring success by incident count alone.** Fewer *reported* incidents can mean less honesty. Track declaration latency, MTTD, MTTR, recurrence *class*, action completion.\n+- **Review optional after \"small\" incidents.** Small incidents are cheap rehearsals of the big ones' causes; review them, lighter but present.\n+- **Hero dependency.** If only one person can mitigate, your response plan is one resignation away from failure. Rotate, document, drill — share the knowledge the incident generates.",
