            "Incidents are run as checklists, not heroics: declare early by user impact, staff the roles (an incident commander who coordinates rather than codes, a scribe from minute zero, a comms channel on cadence), mitigate first with rehearsed levers — rollback by digest, operational flags, edge limits, degradation — while diagnosing in parallel, and resolve when health is restored and stable, not when the cause is found. The post-incident review converts the incident into systemic fixes: a timeline from the scribe, a cause chain that terminates in process and design rather than people, a small set of owned and dated action items each tied to a why, protected things that went well, and detection improvements as first-class deliverables. Error budgets make the loop self-funding — spent budget freezes risky change until the fixes ship — and the measures that prove improvement are MTTD, MTTR, recurrence class and action completion. Blamelessness is the mechanism, not the manners: systems that hide incidents cannot fix them.",
